# **The retry cadence** (bl-4e3c) — and it is the whole of the reconciler's
# retry mechanism. `reconcile.sh` never sleeps and looks again; it answers once
# and exits, and a deferred upgrade is simply re-asked here. That is the same
# discipline `verify.sh` states as "one bounded sleep, no polling loop", and it
# is what keeps a busy engine from being polled by a script that outlives it.
#
# Fifteen minutes, because the two costs bracket it: the interval is how long a
# box lags a release in the worst case AND how long an upgrade waits after the
# turn that deferred it ends, while every fire spends one anonymous registry
# read. A shorter cadence buys minutes of lag for a lot of reads; a longer one
# makes "it deployed" a thing an operator waits an hour to see.
#
# `OnBootSec` is ten minutes rather than immediate: a box that just rebooted is
# starting its engine, its container daemon and whatever else, and the reconcile
# pass has no deadline that competes with any of that.
#
# `RandomizedDelaySec` because a fleet seated from one checkout would otherwise
# ask the registry the same question at the same second forever.
[Unit]
Description=yog engine reconcile timer (unattended CD)
Documentation=https://github.com/mudbungie/yog

[Timer]
OnBootSec=10min
OnUnitActiveSec=15min
RandomizedDelaySec=5min
Unit=yog-reconcile.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
